# ─── secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This repository is public and publishes signed packages. Same list as the
# kernel repo, for the same reason: a .gitignore is only useful for the mistake
# you have not made yet.
*.asc
*.gpg
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.key
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
handheld-repo-*
*passphrase*
*secret*
*credentials*
rclone.conf
.rclone.conf
.aws/

# The blanket *.env is for stray credential dumps, so the two kinds of .env this
# project actually uses have to be named back in. Copied from the kernel repo,
# where the only exception was sources.env; here it silently swallowed repo.env
# and every packages/*/upstream.env -- i.e. the entire upstream-tracking
# configuration -- and the loss is invisible until something reads a default.
.env
.env.*
*.env
!repo.env
!packages/*/upstream.env
*.local.*

# ─── build output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
out/
.build/
.cache/
.ccache/
*.pkg.tar.zst
*.pkg.tar.zst.sig
*.pkg.tar.xz
*.pkg.tar.xz.sig

# ─── makepkg run by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# scripts/build-in-container.sh sets BUILDDIR and SRCDEST so makepkg never writes
# inside packages/, and that used to be the argument for having no
# packages/*/src/ or packages/*/pkg/ rules here at all.
#
# The argument only covers the container path. Running `makepkg` directly in a
# package directory is the obvious thing to do when debugging ONE package, and
# it gets makepkg's defaults instead: src/ (an extracted upstream tree), pkg/ (a
# staged $pkgdir, root-owned files and all) and the downloaded source tarball
# all land in packages/<pkg>/. That is how packages/mangohud/ ended up with all
# three untracked, one `git add -A` away from being committed.
#
# src/ needs the exception below, because the reason those rules were called
# "actively wrong" is real: decky-plugin-handheld-control is first-party and its
# src/ is TypeScript that HAS to be committed. Ignoring a directory and then
# negating that same directory does work -- what git cannot do is re-include a
# file whose parent directory stayed excluded, and the negation here is on the
# directory itself. (index.tsx is already tracked, so it would survive either
# way; the negation is what keeps a NEWLY added .tsx from being invisible to
# `git add`.)
packages/*/pkg/
packages/*/src/
!packages/decky-plugin-handheld-control/src/

# Sources makepkg downloaded, same in-place case as above. No package here
# vendors its own tarball -- every one of them fetches, so anything matching
# this is build input, not content.
packages/*/*.tar.gz
packages/*/*.tar.xz
packages/*/*.tar.bz2
packages/*/*.tar.zst

# ─── in-place JS build output ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# decky-plugin-handheld-control is first-party, so its PKGBUILD has an empty
# source=() and builds from $startdir directly -- BUILDDIR and SRCDEST cannot
# keep pnpm out of the package directory the way they do for downloaded sources.
#
# pnpm-lock.yaml is ignored rather than committed, which is a decision worth
# knowing about: see the note in that package's directory.
packages/*/node_modules/
packages/*/dist/
packages/*/pnpm-lock.yaml
packages/*/package-lock.json
packages/*/.pnpm/

# ─── fetched, not vendored ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# External patch sets, pulled by scripts/fetch-patch-set.sh at the ref
# pinned in each package's upstream.env. Committing them would make this a quiet
# fork of armada's work; the pinned commit is the reviewable part.
packages/*/patch-set/

# ─── generated ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.build-report.md
.upstream-report.md

packages/gtk2/

# ─── editor / OS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/
